//// exmp 1 witout virtual ////

class base { 
    int arr[10];     
}; 
class b1: public base { }; 

class b2: public base { }; 

class derived: public b1, public b2 {}; 

sizeof(derived); ------------------> 80 (If integer takes 4 bytes)

//// exmp 1 with virtual ////

class b1 : virtual public base {};

class b2 : virtual public base {};

sizeof(derived); ------------------> 56 (16 extra bytes are for bookkeeping information stored by the compiler.)

---> b1 and b2 both inherit from class base, two copies of class base are there in class derived. 
     This kind of inheritance without virtual causes wastage of space and ambiguities. 
     virtual base classes are used to save space and avoid ambiguities in such cases.


