### (14_7.1) ###
class Test1 { };

class Test2 {
public:
    operator Test1() { return t1; }
    operator int() { return x; }
};

void fun ( int x)  { };
void fun ( Test1 t ) { };

from main -
Test2 t;
fun(t);
---> The compiler sees:
     t is a Test2.
     But fun is overloaded for int and Test1.
     Test2 can be implicitly converted to both int and Test1.

---> Compiler Error There are two conversion operators defined in the Test2 class. 
     So Test2 objects can automatically be converted to both int and Test1. 
     Therefore, the function call fun(t) is ambiguous as there are two functions void fun(int ) and void fun(Test1 ), 
     compiler has no way to decide which function to call. In general, conversion operators must be overloaded carefully as they may lead to ambiguity. 

How to fix the ambiguity
1. Explicit cast when calling:
   
    fun((int)t);     // calls fun(int)
    fun((Test1)t);   // calls fun(Test1)

2. Remove one conversion operator (if not needed).
3. Make conversion explicit (C++11+):
    
    explicit operator int() { return x; }
  
  Then it won’t be used implicitly, only with a cast.
